<Project>
  <!--
    dev-qual's C# baseline, part two: the one setting that must be
    evaluated after the project's own properties and package references
    are in scope, so it belongs in Directory.Build.targets rather than
    Directory.Build.props (see Baseline.props for the rest of the
    baseline, and why it must be split this way).

    To adopt, import this file from the project's own
    Directory.Build.targets (create one at the solution/repo root if it
    doesn't exist):

      <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)dev-qual/guidance/languages/csharp/tools/Baseline.targets" />

    Adjust the path to wherever dev-qual is checked out relative to the
    importing file. Verify adoption with:

      dotnet run tools/check-baseline.cs, passing the project dir with the
      project option; see its help for the exact flag.

    NB. Baseline.props turns a missing `///` comment on a public member
    into a build error (CS1591), via GenerateDocumentationFile plus
    TreatWarningsAsErrors. Test projects are exempted from just that one
    warning here, conditioned on `IsTestProject` — a property that
    Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk sets (referenced by the MSTest/NUnit/xUnit
    SDKs). Directory.Build.props is imported before the project body and
    its package references, while `IsTestProject` is set from a package's
    own props file, imported later; Directory.Build.targets is imported
    after all of that, so this condition sees the real value.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(IsTestProject)' == 'true'">
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
